## Setup Environment

### Install Erlang and Elixir

This project requires **Erlang** and **Elixir**. The specific versions are defined in the `.tool-versions` file. The easiest way to install them is with a version manager such as [`asdf`](https://asdf-vm.com/) or [`mise`](https://mise.jdx.dev/).

```sh
asdf install
# or
mise install
```

> **Note:** This step is not necessary if you plan to run the server using [Docker Compose](#start-the-server-with-docker-compose).

### Configure Application

In order to configure the environment variables, create a `.env` file in the project root directory, using the `.env.sample` file as template:

```sh
cp .env.sample .env
```

Then edit `.env` with the actual credentials for the desired environment. See [Environment Variables](#environment-variables) for the complete reference.

## Start the Server

### Start the Server with Elixir

1. Export the environment variables to the current shell session:

    ```sh
    set -a && source .env && set +a
    ```

2. Install dependencies and setup database:

    ```sh
    mix setup
    ```

3. Start server (Standard mode):

    ```sh
    mix phx.server
    ```

    If an Elixir Interactive Shell is needed, start the server with:

    ```sh
    iex -S mix phx.server
    ```

    The available endpoints will depend on the `MIX_ENV` value. See [Application Interfaces](#application-interfaces) for URLs.

### Start the Server with Docker Compose

1. Start the application:

    ```sh
    docker compose up --build
    ```

    > **Note:** First build may take several minutes depending on the internet connection.

2. Stop the application:

    Press `Ctrl+C` in the terminal, or run:

    ```sh
    docker compose down
    ```

## Test the Code

1. Run automated unit tests:

    ```sh
    mix test
    ```

    For all available command line options, see the [Mix.Tasks.Test](https://hexdocs.pm/mix/Mix.Tasks.Test.html#module-command-line-options) documentation.

2. Generate testing and coverage reports:

    ```sh
<%= "    " <> @coverage_command %>
    ```
<%= if @exdoc do %>
## Browse the Documentation

Generate the ExDoc HTML documentation with:

```sh
mix docs
```

The output is written to `doc/`. Open `index.html` in a browser to navigate the docs without running the server. The same content is also served at <http://localhost:<%= @port %>/dev/docs/> when the server is running.

> **Note:** The `/dev/docs/` endpoint is only available in development deployments.

`doc/llms.txt` contains a flat index of every module and function — useful as context for LLM-assisted development.
<% end %>
